# Subtractive clock connective: the hour, then "minus", then the fraction or
# the minutes.  Same Arabic borrowing as ar/clock_dir_to.voc ("إلا").
#
# "ɣir" is the standard spelling.  @athmanemokraoui, a native Kabyle speaker,
# answering on https://github.com/TigreGotico/chronologia/pull/846#issuecomment-5573978767:
#   "Minus on the clock is 'ɣir' -- standard INALCO Kabyle orthography does
#    not use macrons or dots on standard vowels/liquids for this loan."
ɣir
# "ɣiṛ" is the underdotted variant the Kabyle sentences on Tatoeba write
# (native contributors), with their English pairs:
#   "Ha-tt-an d ttmanya ɣiṛ ṛṛbeɛ."  -- "It's a quarter to eight."
#   "Ha-tt-an d lweḥda ɣiṛ ṛṛbeɛ."   -- "It's a quarter to one."
#   "Ha-tt-an d ssaɛtin ɣiṛ ṛṛbeɛ."  -- "It's quarter to two."
#   "Ha-tt-an d tmenya ɣir ɛecra."   -- "It's ten to eight."
# The two sources disagree on the underdot and both surfaces stay readable:
# a parser accepts every attested variation, and someone will type this one.
# The standard form to WRITE is "ɣir" above.  The last pair also shows the
# slot after the connective takes plain minutes, not only the quarter -- a
# reading @athmanemokraoui confirms ("d lɛecṛa ɣiṛ xemsa" for five to ten,
# answer 7 of the same comment).
ɣiṛ
